  "final_text": "All four steps are done.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' covers the index `kibana_sample_data_flights`, and its time field is `timestamp`. The fields used here are `Carrier` (string) and `Cancelled` (true/false). Others include `FlightDelay`, `FlightDelayMin`, `AvgTicketPrice`, `Origin*`/`Dest*`, `DistanceKilometers` and `dayOfWeek`.\n2. **Dashboard:** I created 'S4-dfd5d395 Flights' (id `s4-dfd5d395-flights-8a252994de6a`) with two panels:\n   - a line chart of flight count over `timestamp`\n   - a bar chart of flight count by `Carrier` (top 10 carriers)\n3. **Alert rule:** I created 'S4-dfd5d395 Cancelled flights' (id `4bece4b7-4118-4151-8ad1-97d0fa76f195`). It is an Elasticsearch query rule that checks every minute for flights where `Cancelled` is true and fires when there are more than 10 in a 5-minute window. It was created already enabled, so I didn't need to turn it on separately.\n4. **Panel edit and check:** I gave panel 1 the title \"Flights by carrier\". I'd already used that title when creating the dashboard, so this update didn't change what you see. Reading the rule back shows it is enabled, with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
